memory-writer — phase report

RECONSTRUCTED, and it matters that you know that. The original report for this
phase does not exist: `run_claude` truncates the phase log on every retry, this
phase failed twice (07:15, 07:30) and was interrupted mid-way through its third
attempt, so whatever it had to say went with it. Everything below was verified
directly against the tree afterwards rather than copied from that agent's
account — the commits are its work, the numbers and the live check are not.


WHAT IS THERE

Nine modules under `tars/memory/`, 3090 lines, committed across
fa912fa..68bdf5f:

  types.py       the schema PHASE 6's rules imply — the four kinds, the two
                 thresholds, and the dispositions
  transcript.py  a reader that preserves whose voice a turn was in, because
                 "the user said we must" and "I decided we must" are not the
                 same claim
  domains.py     tagging from markers that actually appear in the text, plus a
                 project-supplied map at `.tars/memory/domains.json`
  signals.py     cue detectors for the four memory-worthy kinds
  propose.py     the REFLECT pass: transcript in, scored candidates out
  store.py       partitioned JSON, a review queue, and marks rather than deletes
  cli.py         `tars-memory` — reflect / recall / pending / approve / reject
  __init__.py    the package surface: reflect_on, remember, recall

Nothing is half-written; the tree was clean at the interruption, so the third
attempt's last act (68bdf5f, 07:43) was a commit rather than an edit.


VERIFICATION

  memory tests          277 passed
  whole suite           848 passed, 2 skipped
  ruff / format / mypy  clean on every file in tars/memory

Benchmark against a hand-labelled corpus of 76 statements (45 memory-worthy, 31
not), every floor met:

  detector precision  0.93   (floor 0.80)
  detector recall     0.87   (floor 0.70)
  kind accuracy       0.97   (floor 0.85)
  store precision     1.00   (floor 0.80)
  store recall        0.87

The scorecard names its 6 misses, 3 false positives and 1 wrong-kind case rather
than reporting a single number. `python tests/test_memory_benchmark.py` prints
it.

Live end-to-end check, run by hand on a four-turn transcript rather than trusted
from the tests: `reflect` produced one commit-grade and one review-grade
candidate with per-signal score breakdowns, and correctly dropped both "ran the
tests, all passed" and a question. `--write` partitioned into
`.tars/memory/{providers,build,pending}.json`; `pending`, `recall --domain
providers`, `approve` and `reject` all behave as documented, and the exit codes
(0 ok, 1 needs a human, 2 usage, 3 nothing memory-worthy) are real.

Against PHASE 6's rules specifically: domains are tagged only from explicit
evidence, superseded entries are marked and stay readable but stop coming back
from `recall`, low-confidence candidates go to a queue `recall` cannot see, and
an empty report says so instead of reaching for something. All four are covered
by tests.


WHAT THE PHASE DID NOT DO, AND WHAT WAS DONE ABOUT IT

  - No checkpoint commit. Added now.
  - No entry in run/summary.log. Added now.
  - README.md left stale: it documented neither `tars-memory` nor the module.
    Regenerated with the tool itself; `tars-readme . --check` now exits 0.
  - Console scripts were declared in pyproject but never installed, so
    `tars-memory` did not exist as a command and the CLI's own help told you to
    run `tars-memory approve <ID>`. `pip install -e .` fixed that; all three
    commands now resolve.


WHAT IS STILL MISSING FROM THIS MODULE

Semantic recall. The spec's memory section asks for the existing vector DB on
the embedded side with JSON or SQLite for the structured side; only the
structured side was built. `recall` matches on domain and kind exactly, so
ORIENT cannot ask "what do we know that is *related* to this task". That is a
real gap against the spec, not an oversight in the code that exists, and it is
big enough to be its own phase.
